+# Summary
+
+## What changed
+
+Only `relnotes/changelog.py` was modified.
+
+- The release heading regex now accepts a heading without a date, so `## [Unreleased]` no longer makes the parser crash on a `None` match. A `## ` line that is not a release heading is treated as body text instead of crashing.
+- HTML comments are removed from the body, both inline (`- A bug. <!-- issue 7 -->`) and spanning several lines. A line that had a comment removed loses any whitespace left at its end.
+- Runs of blank lines in the body collapse to one blank line, and leading and trailing blank lines are dropped.
+- The heading is `# <version> (<date>)` for dated releases and `# Unreleased` when the section has no date.
+- `LookupError` for an unknown version is unchanged, and `__main__.py` still turns it into a message and exit code 1.
+
+## What was verified
+
+- `python -m unittest -v`: all 4 tests pass (previously all 4 errored with `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'group'`).
+- `python -m relnotes CHANGELOG.md 2.4.0` now prints, with no comments and no double blank lines:
+
+```
+# 2.4.0 (2024-05-02)
+
+### Added
+- Support for `## [Unreleased]` sections.
+- `--output` flag for writing notes to a file.
+
+### Fixed
+- Dates with single-digit days are parsed correctly.
+
+### Changed
+- Minimum supported Python is now 3.10.
+```
+
+- `python -m relnotes CHANGELOG.md Unreleased` prints `# Unreleased` followed by the Added section.
+- `python -m relnotes CHANGELOG.md 2.3.1` renders correctly.
+- `python -m relnotes CHANGELOG.md 9.9.9` prints `version 9.9.9 not found in CHANGELOG.md` and exits 1.
+- A copy of `CHANGELOG.md` converted to CRLF line endings renders byte-for-byte the same 2.4.0 notes as the LF original.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- The README does not say whether a comment that spans two release headings should be allowed. Comments are stripped per release body, so such a comment would not swallow the following heading. The real changelog has no such case.
+- The date pattern accepts single-digit months and days (`2024-5-2`) as well as the zero-padded form, since the 2.4.0 notes mention that. Dates are passed through to the heading unchanged, not normalised.
+- No new unit tests were added; the existing four cover the README rules and now pass.
